{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 13, "model_name": "stockmark/stockmark-2-100b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a compelling and thought-provoking film that delves into the origins and rise of facebook, as well as the complex human drama surrounding its founder mark zuckerberg. the movie is directed by frederick mcdormand and stars jesse eisenberg as zuckerberg, along with andrew garfield, josh charles, and eddie redmayne in supporting roles.\n\nthe film opens with zuckerberg being sued by his former friend and co-founder edward saverin, played by redmayne, which serves as a framing device for the narrative. the story then flashes back to zuckerberg's time at harvard university, where he creates facebook from a simple idea into a global phenomenon. the movie explores the legal battles, personal betrayals, and ethical dilemmas that emerge as zuckerberg's creation gains popularity.\n\neisenberg's portrayal of zuckerberg is both captivating and nuanced. he captures the character's brilliance, social awkwardness, and relentless ambition. the supporting cast is equally strong, with garfield as zuckerberg's rival and charles as a harvard law professor providing additional complexity to the story.\n\nthe social network is not just a biopic; it is a commentary on the power and influence of social media in our lives. the script, written by auntie goldman, is sharp and witty, with dialogue that feels authentic to the characters and the time period. the film's pacing is excellent, keeping the audience engaged throughout the 120-minute runtime.\n\nvisually, the movie is stunning. the cinematography, by erik vincent, captures the harvard campus and the early days of facebook with a raw and gritty style. the editing, by kelly lough, is seamless, moving between different time periods and storylines with ease.\n\nthe score, composed by tyler bates, is haunting and evocative, adding to the emotional weight of the narrative. the music perfectly complements the film's tone, whether it's the tension of a legal battle or the nostalgia of zuckerberg's college days.\n\nthe social network is a film that will leave you thinking long after the credits roll. it is a compelling drama about the cost of success and the impact of technology on our society. while it may not be entirely historically accurate, it is a powerful and engaging story that resonates with audiences. the film's exploration of fame, fortune, and the price of innovation makes it a must-watch for anyone interested in the intersection of technology and human nature.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.12809905409812927}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -0.006481222342699766}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -0.0004832768754567951}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.08662274479866028}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.1506062000989914}, {"token": " compelling", "logprob": -1.3879568576812744}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.9085878729820251}, {"token": " thought", "logprob": -0.9967272877693176}, {"token": "-provoking", "logprob": -0.06121998652815819}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -0.055569734424352646}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.12317843735218048}, {"token": " delves", "logprob": -0.5899597406387329}, {"token": " into", "logprob": -0.026470540091395378}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.0008068405441008508}, {"token": " origins", "logprob": -1.702815294265747}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.977438747882843}, {"token": " rise", "logprob": -1.6043939590454102}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.0012422234285622835}, {"token": " face", "logprob": -0.006517937406897545}, {"token": "book", "logprob": -2.3483953555114567e-05}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.6602874398231506}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -1.4888840913772583}, {"token": " well", "logprob": -0.1326385736465454}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -0.00019012074335478246}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.027021145448088646}, {"token": " complex", "logprob": -0.7035207748413086}, {"token": " h